>     /etc/profile.d/pica8.sh


Fisrt time you run a container:

    $./run.sh /path/to/picos/code_dir

After starting finish, you will get a bash shell.

When you check the `/build` directory, will find out that is `code_dir`.

Docker will mount the host directory(as *code_dir*) onto the container /build.

The login user is 'build', a 'virtual' user for container use only to simplify build management.

The permission of all your files still the same as the host system.

Press 'Enter' to get into it. Input your user password if asked for.

Run `su build` to work as user `build` mapped to your own account.

A file name of the container name will be generated for container check.

You can check the latest Container ID from file `.docker.run`.

    # su build
```sh
cd /build/
./build.sh BOXNAME
```

Now you can use container as a build environment with tool-chains built in.

When you finish the product building, just leave shell with exit. Then container will hibernate.
The working directory holds the build result.

When container is running, you can check the log and status of building.

```sh
docker ps -a|grep $USER
```

Display last 24 lines logs of specified container name.

```sh
docker logs -t --tail 24 CONTAINER
```

List container status and mount points.

```sh
{% raw %}
$ docker ps --format "table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}\\t{{.Mounts}}"
{% endraw %}
```

List container CPU and memory usage.

```sh
{%- raw -%}
docker stats --no-stream --format "table {{.Name}}\t{{.CPUPerc}}\t{{.MemUsage}}"
{% endraw %}
```

After you left any container, you can restart and attach to it.

```sh
docker start CONTAINER
docker attach CONTAINER
```

Then press 'Enter' to get into it.

The docker administrators will clean containers regularly.

When container is running, you can run docker 'exec' command to build.

```sh
sudo docker exec -it CONTAINER /bin/bash
su build
cd /build
./build.sh prontoBOX
```

